Sweet Auction - Minimal Realtime Auction App
============================================

Sweet Auction is a small, production-ready Node.js app that lets a single seller run one live auction for sweets while buyers place bids with quarter-dollar increments. It uses SQLite for durable storage and Server-Sent Events (SSE) for realtime updates without complex infrastructure.

Features
--------

- Single active auction with title, description, photo, starting price, and optional end time.
- Realtime bid updates over SSE; buyers see current prices and history without refreshing.
- Bid increments fixed at $0.25; server enforces ordering and prevents races with SQLite transactions.
- Buyers save a display name locally (no accounts) and can place quick or custom bids.
- Seller unlocks admin tools with an env-configured passcode to create/update/end auctions and inspect bid logs.
- Clean, responsive UI built with vanilla HTML/CSS/JS.

Stack
-----

- **Runtime:** Node.js 20+
- **Server:** Express + better-sqlite3 (synchronous)
- **Realtime:** Server-Sent Events
- **Database:** SQLite (prices stored as integer cents)
- **Frontend:** Static HTML/CSS/JS (no frameworks)

Getting Started
---------------

```bash
git clone <repo>
cd workspace
npm install
echo "ADMIN_PASS=changeme" > .env
npm run start
# open http://localhost:3000
```

During development you can use hot reload:

```bash
npm run dev
```

Configuration
-------------

Environment variables (via `.env`):

- `ADMIN_PASS` – Required passcode for seller routes. Keep this secret in production.
- `PORT` – Optional. Defaults to `3000`.

Project Structure
-----------------

```
server/
  db.js          # SQLite setup & helper queries
  index.js       # Express app, routes, SSE
  sse.js         # SSE client registry and broadcaster
public/
  index.html     # Single-page auction UI
  styles.css     # Minimal responsive styling
  app.js         # Frontend logic (buyers + admin)
data/            # SQLite database file (created automatically, gitignored)
```

Key Workflows
-------------

- **Creating/Editing Auctions:** Unlock seller controls with the admin pass, fill the form, and submit. Starting prices and all bids are stored in cents and rounded to $0.25 increments.
- **Placing Bids:** Buyers enter a display name once (stored in `localStorage`) and can use quick buttons or custom amounts. The server validates increments, ensures bids exceed the current price, and rejects late bids.
- **Realtime Stream:** `/api/stream` uses SSE, broadcasting `state`, `bid`, and `end` events to all connected clients with a heartbeat every 25s.
- **Ending Auctions:** Sellers can end the auction manually, instantly locking out new bids and notifying clients.

API Overview
------------

- `GET /api/auction` – Current auction snapshot with highest bid and bidder.
- `GET /api/bids?limit=20` – Recent bids for the latest auction.
- `POST /api/bid` – Place a bid (`{ bidderName, amountCents }`).
- `GET /api/stream` – SSE feed (`state`, `bid`, `end` events).
- `POST /api/admin/auction` *(admin)* – Create a new active auction.
- `PATCH /api/admin/auction` *(admin)* – Update the current auction.
- `POST /api/admin/end` *(admin)* – End the current auction.
- `GET /api/admin/bids` *(admin)* – Full bid log for the latest auction.

Admin requests must include the header `x-admin-pass: <ADMIN_PASS>` (or a `Bearer` token) and will fail with `401` if the passcode is invalid.

Operational Notes
-----------------

- The database file lives in `data/auction.db` and is created automatically; ensure the process has write permissions in production.
- The server uses SQLite transactions to guarantee bid ordering when multiple bids arrive nearly simultaneously.
- SSE clients receive a heartbeat comment every 25 seconds to keep proxies from timing out the connection.
- The frontend rounds all custom bid inputs up to the next $0.25 step, but the server remains the source of truth.

Troubleshooting
---------------

- **Admin unlock fails:** Confirm `ADMIN_PASS` is set and matches the value in `.env`, then restart the server.
- **No realtime updates:** Ensure the browser keeps the tab open, check console/network logs, and verify reverse proxies allow streaming responses.
- **SQLite file locked:** Stop other processes using the DB. For containerized deployments, mount `data/` on persistent storage.

